WEBVTT

00:03.731 --> 00:04.821
I think the best place to look
would be Reid's book.

00:04.901 --> 00:06.731
Why don't we all
search the archive?

00:06.821 --> 00:09.781
So what you're suggesting is
we get a hold of Reid's book

00:09.861 --> 00:12.621
and use it as a guide
to defeating him and passing,

00:12.701 --> 00:13.621
I suppose.

00:13.701 --> 00:15.041
Mm.

00:15.121 --> 00:16.541
If we read
his Book of the Dead,

00:16.621 --> 00:18.411
we'll find out
how his life ended.

00:18.501 --> 00:20.291
He's no exception to the rule.

00:20.371 --> 00:22.501
When you put it that way,
it does make sense.

00:22.581 --> 00:24.421
That plan is unorthodox.

00:24.501 --> 00:26.211
It's painfully Barasu.

00:26.301 --> 00:28.011
Yes, it is.

00:28.091 --> 00:29.381
Such bamboozlement.

00:29.471 --> 00:31.181
It's what Subaru's
really good at.

00:31.261 --> 00:32.641
Well, there's a word
you don't hear anymore

00:32.721 --> 00:34.221
this day and age.

00:34.301 --> 00:38.021
But first off, do we know
beyond a shadow of a doubt

00:38.101 --> 00:40.851
that Reid Astrea's book
is in the archive?

00:40.941 --> 00:42.521
At this point,
all we know for certain

00:42.601 --> 00:45.361
is that he was alive
400 or so years ago.

00:45.441 --> 00:47.361
Then shouldn't it go without
saying that him still being

00:47.441 --> 00:49.151
alive today is impossible?

00:49.241 --> 00:51.071
Dude's got to be dead
as a doornail.

00:51.151 --> 00:52.321
[BEATRICE]
What a bold assumption.

00:52.411 --> 00:54.031
Huh?

00:54.121 --> 00:56.541
Betty has lived that long
despite appearances, I suppose.

00:56.621 --> 00:59.621
And I've been alive
about 100, I think.

00:59.711 --> 01:02.001
While we're on the subject,
it's been about 400 years

01:02.081 --> 01:03.621
since I came into being.

01:03.711 --> 01:07.001
And I've been waiting around
on my lonesome for 400 years.

01:07.091 --> 01:09.131
The average age
of the party just skyrocketed!

01:09.211 --> 01:10.511
Emilia, you, too?!

01:12.011 --> 01:14.341
Even so,
given his personality,

01:14.431 --> 01:17.471
it's highly unlikely
he is of a long-lived species.

01:17.561 --> 01:19.101
His personality?

01:19.181 --> 01:21.811
You mean
how he's really energetic?

01:21.891 --> 01:23.231
Rather than energetic,

01:23.311 --> 01:26.231
I'd say completely uninhibited,
perhaps.

01:26.321 --> 01:28.571
Even if he has lived
that long,

01:28.651 --> 01:30.651
it's hard to believe
he'd spend 400 years

01:30.741 --> 01:32.401
sitting around in this tower.

01:32.491 --> 01:33.911
Good point.

01:33.991 --> 01:36.411
Based on the things I
know about Reid Astrea,

01:36.491 --> 01:38.541
he doesn't strike me
as a man who'd willingly stay

01:38.621 --> 01:40.411
in one place for long.

01:42.161 --> 01:43.831
All right,
so are we in agreement

01:43.911 --> 01:46.421
that Reid is the most lively
dead man to ever exist?

01:46.501 --> 01:47.631
[ALL] Hmm.

01:48.421 --> 01:50.421
Well then.

01:50.501 --> 01:51.841
If that's what it takes
to move forward

01:51.921 --> 01:55.011
and clear the tower,
let's do it.

01:58.891 --> 02:01.431
[footsteps]

02:04.811 --> 02:05.981
Something wrong?

02:06.061 --> 02:07.441
Still against the idea?

02:07.521 --> 02:08.611
No.

02:08.691 --> 02:10.521
There's no other way
out of this.

02:10.611 --> 02:13.821
And I acknowledge that the plan
you came up with has merit.

02:13.901 --> 02:16.071
But you have your doubts
about it anyway.

02:17.491 --> 02:20.031
My lack of confidence
is a personal problem.

02:20.121 --> 02:21.621
Please don't worry about it.

02:24.121 --> 02:25.211
Ram?

02:27.461 --> 02:29.671
Could you look after
the others for a bit?

02:34.381 --> 02:36.721
So, you wanted a word?

02:38.931 --> 02:40.051
Yeah.

02:40.141 --> 02:41.931
Are you intending
to defeat Reid?

02:44.311 --> 02:45.311
Subaru.

02:45.391 --> 02:46.441
You--

02:46.521 --> 02:48.021
Sorry, man.

02:48.111 --> 02:50.271
The truth is, I want to
help you all that I can.

02:50.361 --> 02:52.401
I'd love to take the time
to get your wounded heart

02:52.481 --> 02:53.741
fixed up, really.

02:53.821 --> 02:56.401
But we don't have
that kind of time.

02:56.491 --> 02:58.861
So I'm going to be
straight with you, Julius.

02:58.951 --> 03:01.621
[sighs]

03:01.701 --> 03:03.621
Please.

03:03.701 --> 03:06.291
[SUBARU] That's one thing,
and this is another.

03:06.371 --> 03:07.871
Huh?

03:07.961 --> 03:10.171
You probably feel
uncomfortable dealing with me,

03:10.251 --> 03:11.801
and I understand.

03:11.881 --> 03:14.051
Yesterday's Subaru must
have done something to you

03:14.131 --> 03:15.631
that I don't know about.

03:15.721 --> 03:18.931
But that has nothing to do
with who I am right now.

03:20.851 --> 03:22.351
Out of everyone
in our party,

03:22.431 --> 03:24.681
you're definitely
the strongest.

03:24.771 --> 03:26.981
So even if we find
the perfect strategy guide

03:27.061 --> 03:29.691
to conquering him, ultimately,
we'll still need you

03:29.771 --> 03:31.861
to be the one to fight him.

03:31.941 --> 03:34.941
But I have already lost to him
two times now.

03:35.031 --> 03:36.991
I know.

03:38.281 --> 03:40.701
But you can win next time.

03:42.121 --> 03:43.831
I didn't get to see
how it ended.

03:43.911 --> 03:46.291
I remember nothing
before yesterday.

03:46.371 --> 03:49.421
So you see, I've never
seen you lose to Reid,

03:49.501 --> 03:51.711
and there's no reason for me
to think that you would.

03:53.301 --> 03:55.461
What you're lacking here
isn't courage, man.

03:55.551 --> 03:58.721
[soft music]

03:58.801 --> 04:00.551
It's the will to seize victory.

04:00.641 --> 04:02.051
It's determination.

04:02.141 --> 04:03.261
Because courage?

04:03.351 --> 04:05.891
You've got all you need
right here.

04:05.971 --> 04:07.481
So I'm leaving this to you.

04:07.561 --> 04:09.481
You'll beat Reid Astrea.

04:09.561 --> 04:10.771
You should be the one
to take down

04:10.851 --> 04:12.271
our most challenging enemy.

04:12.361 --> 04:15.691
♪ ♪

04:15.781 --> 04:18.491
You can't remember a single
thing before yesterday.

04:18.571 --> 04:21.361
How can you have
such high expectations?

04:21.451 --> 04:24.081
Well, that's because,
well, you know,

04:24.161 --> 04:26.621
it's just the way
that I see you.

04:26.701 --> 04:29.291
Everything about you
gives me that hope, Julius.

04:30.791 --> 04:31.631
I see.

04:33.171 --> 04:36.801
So it was my appearance that
gave you that impression.

04:36.881 --> 04:40.841
I may have been forgotten, my
master's existence uncertain.

04:40.931 --> 04:44.051
But despite all of that, I
haven't lost all of the things

04:44.141 --> 04:46.891
that I've been cultivating
over my entire life.

04:46.971 --> 04:48.521
That's what you mean, isn't it?

04:48.601 --> 04:51.521
I wasn't clever enough to sum
it up like that, but yeah.

04:51.601 --> 04:53.521
You filled with determination?

04:53.601 --> 04:54.611
Perhaps.

04:54.691 --> 04:55.651
We'll see.

04:55.731 --> 04:57.441
Your words are idealistic.

04:57.531 --> 04:59.441
Their foundation
is truly lacking.

04:59.531 --> 05:00.951
Oh, come on, man.

05:01.031 --> 05:06.491
However, that is one thing,
and this is another.

05:10.211 --> 05:11.211
Subaru, look!

05:11.291 --> 05:13.171
We found Reid's book already.

05:13.251 --> 05:14.501
For real?

05:16.041 --> 05:18.001
Meili was the one
who found it, actually.

05:18.091 --> 05:19.551
She did good, huh?

05:19.631 --> 05:21.011
It was nothing.

05:21.091 --> 05:22.881
I just happened to look over
and randomly see it,

05:22.971 --> 05:23.931
that's all.

05:24.011 --> 05:25.181
Don't be silly.

05:25.261 --> 05:26.431
That's a real accomplishment.

05:26.511 --> 05:28.521
It's OK to be loud and proud.

05:28.601 --> 05:29.891
Well done, Meili.

05:29.971 --> 05:31.021
You're messing up my hair.

05:31.101 --> 05:31.891
Knock it off.

05:33.441 --> 05:37.361
Although, if you found it
by chance, then that means--

05:37.441 --> 05:39.691
Unfortunately,
we haven't figured out

05:39.781 --> 05:41.651
how the archive functions.

05:41.741 --> 05:43.201
Disappointing.

05:43.281 --> 05:45.071
There were a few other
books I would have really

05:45.161 --> 05:46.581
liked to have found too.

05:47.661 --> 05:48.581
Subaru?

05:48.661 --> 05:50.081
You mean?

05:50.161 --> 05:52.501
I told you,
I'm not cutting corners

05:52.581 --> 05:55.131
when it comes to making sure
you grow up healthy and strong.

05:58.961 --> 06:01.921
So while I was gone,
nobody tried to sneak a peek

06:02.011 --> 06:03.551
at Reid's book, did they?

06:03.631 --> 06:06.261
Obviously, if someone else
were to carelessly read a book

06:06.341 --> 06:07.931
and end up losing
their memories too,

06:08.011 --> 06:09.311
we'd be in trouble.

06:09.391 --> 06:11.601
I'll just say this--
we don't know for sure

06:11.681 --> 06:15.731
that it was the book that left
me like this or something else.

06:15.811 --> 06:17.691
This is going to sound
really self-centered,

06:17.771 --> 06:21.481
but I think I'm the best choice
for reading anything.

06:25.201 --> 06:26.111
Subaru?

06:26.201 --> 06:29.281
[soft music]

06:29.371 --> 06:30.991
We're doing this as a team.

06:31.081 --> 06:32.751
That means
every single one of us

06:32.831 --> 06:35.501
has our own unique role
to play to make this a success.

06:35.581 --> 06:37.631
Julius is in charge
of fighting.

06:37.711 --> 06:40.091
Foxidna is the brains
of the operation.

06:40.171 --> 06:42.011
Ram's our resident
verbal abuser.

06:42.091 --> 06:43.801
Meili's task is to be adorable.

06:43.881 --> 06:45.971
Beatrice's is also
to be adorable.

06:46.051 --> 06:48.091
Shaula does the very
necessary gravure scenes.

06:48.181 --> 06:50.311
And Emilia's job is to be
the beautiful heroine

06:50.391 --> 06:51.721
of this story.

06:51.811 --> 06:53.931
So that leaves this job
for me to take on.

06:54.021 --> 06:55.891
You know,
almost all of those roles

06:55.981 --> 06:58.021
sounded kind
of gobbledygooky and strange.

06:58.101 --> 06:59.651
That's not true!

06:59.731 --> 07:01.941
I'd do anything for you,
Master.

07:02.021 --> 07:03.361
Goodbye, clothes!

07:03.441 --> 07:05.111
Actually, if you take off
any more than that,

07:05.201 --> 07:06.281
it'll just scare me.

07:06.361 --> 07:08.281
You're doing too much.

07:08.371 --> 07:11.081
Left high and dry.
Poor Shaula.

07:11.161 --> 07:14.871
OK, so anyway, I'm going to
be the one to read this book.

07:14.961 --> 07:15.911
Do we agree?

07:16.001 --> 07:17.161
Mm-hmm.

07:18.671 --> 07:20.541
You'd better come back to us.

07:20.631 --> 07:22.711
But no need to promise,
because I'm sure

07:22.801 --> 07:25.011
you'll just end up
breaking it again.

07:25.091 --> 07:27.051
How about if this
does wipe memories,

07:27.131 --> 07:28.511
you freeze me in your ice
and give me

07:28.591 --> 07:30.681
a real stern talking to, huh?

07:30.761 --> 07:32.471
Your thoughts are
always so violent.

07:32.561 --> 07:33.771
Nobody would do that,
I suppose.

07:33.851 --> 07:34.771
Don't worry.

07:34.851 --> 07:36.061
I know that.

07:36.141 --> 07:37.191
You're way too much
of a sweetheart.

07:37.271 --> 07:44.361
♪ ♪

07:47.991 --> 07:49.321
Subaru.

07:49.411 --> 07:51.161
Well, this is it.

07:51.241 --> 07:53.371
I'm afraid I might be
coming home late tonight,

07:53.451 --> 07:55.411
so you can have dinner
without me.

07:56.411 --> 07:57.581
Jerk.

08:00.831 --> 08:04.461
[dramatic music]

08:06.801 --> 08:09.431
[loud whirring]

08:10.341 --> 08:13.181
[gasp]

08:13.261 --> 08:16.391
[eerie sounds]

08:29.281 --> 08:33.911
Is this inside Reid's memory?

08:33.991 --> 08:35.831
[RUI] What do we have here?

08:35.911 --> 08:37.951
It's you again, Mister.

08:38.041 --> 08:39.791
You came back again.

08:43.291 --> 08:44.671
And who might you be?

08:44.751 --> 08:46.631
Wait, no, where are we?

08:46.711 --> 08:48.671
What should I ask first?

08:48.761 --> 08:51.891
[RUI] Oh my goodness, you're
such a greedy guy, Mister.

08:51.971 --> 08:53.261
But we don't hate that.

08:53.341 --> 08:54.641
It's what we prefer.

08:54.721 --> 08:56.681
We can't get enough
of greedy people.

08:56.771 --> 08:59.521
We love them, you know?
[cackles]

08:59.601 --> 09:01.391
Where we're standing
is the lonely,

09:01.481 --> 09:04.691
endlessly white
final resting place for souls.

09:04.771 --> 09:07.191
It's called Od Ragna,
the cradle,

09:07.271 --> 09:09.401
the Corridor of Memories.

09:09.491 --> 09:12.151
[eerie sounds]

09:14.491 --> 09:16.871
Corridor of Memories?

09:16.951 --> 09:18.241
That's right.

09:18.331 --> 09:20.541
The Corridor of Memories.

09:20.621 --> 09:22.411
Let me tell you about us.

09:22.501 --> 09:24.711
We're the Sin Archbishop
in the Witch Cult

09:24.791 --> 09:26.091
representing gluttony.

09:26.171 --> 09:27.881
We're Rui Arneb.

09:27.961 --> 09:29.961
[dramatic music]

09:30.051 --> 09:32.931
We'll only be together
for a short time, I'm afraid.

09:33.011 --> 09:35.351
But it's a real pleasure,
Mister.

09:39.101 --> 09:42.231
First of all, what the heck
is a Witch Cult and a Sin Arch,

09:42.311 --> 09:43.521
or whatever you said?

09:44.901 --> 09:47.481
The Witch Cult is
an interesting little group.

09:47.561 --> 09:49.191
It's a collection
of all the most hated people

09:49.271 --> 09:51.321
in the entire world.

09:51.401 --> 09:54.241
It does sound like the name
of a band of evildoers.

09:54.321 --> 09:57.031
So then you must also be evil.

09:57.121 --> 09:58.451
Oh, don't be like that.

09:58.531 --> 09:59.661
What a mean thing to say

09:59.741 --> 10:02.661
to such a sweet little girl,
Mister.

10:02.751 --> 10:03.961
You have to be careful.

10:04.041 --> 10:05.831
We can get hurt too, you know.

10:05.921 --> 10:07.251
I can't help but notice
how you keep

10:07.341 --> 10:09.421
using we to refer to yourself.

10:09.501 --> 10:11.711
Is that supposed to be
part of your character?

10:11.801 --> 10:14.841
Oh, you shouldn't
worry about that.

10:14.931 --> 10:17.301
We just have a few
too many egos inside.

10:17.391 --> 10:19.931
Gets confusing, so it's not
really clear which one of us

10:20.011 --> 10:21.221
is in charge.

10:21.311 --> 10:23.521
But it is what it is, you know?

10:25.151 --> 10:27.021
It's a precious gift
that my Big Brother

10:27.111 --> 10:28.981
and Brother Dearest gave to us.

10:29.061 --> 10:31.021
It's important
that we accept it,

10:31.111 --> 10:33.191
or we'd be a failure
as a little sister.

10:33.281 --> 10:34.991
Well, aren't you just
the cutest little sister

10:35.071 --> 10:36.661
who loves her big brothers?

10:36.741 --> 10:38.661
I'm an only child,
so I'm kind of jealous.

10:38.741 --> 10:40.491
You sure?

10:40.581 --> 10:42.911
I mean, it's possible that you
have a little brother or sister

10:43.001 --> 10:44.291
that you don't know about.

10:44.371 --> 10:45.911
Can you not say
scary things like that?

10:46.001 --> 10:47.501
I don't even want to go there.

10:47.581 --> 10:49.591
We get it, though, Mister.

10:50.921 --> 10:52.461
You feel guilty
about your parents

10:52.551 --> 10:55.591
like you've done
them wrong, right?

10:55.671 --> 10:58.431
You left your loving parents
without a single word.

10:58.511 --> 11:00.931
You feel like you were
a terrible, ungrateful son,

11:01.011 --> 11:02.771
and now you regret everything.

11:04.061 --> 11:06.311
Now you're wondering
how we know all that.

11:06.391 --> 11:07.601
Well, of course, we do.

11:07.691 --> 11:08.941
Why wouldn't we?

11:09.021 --> 11:11.731
Because you could
scour the entire world,

11:11.821 --> 11:13.651
and you would never
find another person

11:13.741 --> 11:17.701
who understands you even
a fraction as much as we do.

11:17.781 --> 11:19.321
Get off me!

11:19.411 --> 11:21.241
[gasp, thud]

11:24.081 --> 11:25.951
We're just trying
to be helpful.

11:26.041 --> 11:28.001
All we want to do is give you
the peace of mind

11:28.081 --> 11:30.631
we know you need, Mister.

11:30.711 --> 11:32.171
What are you supposed to be?

11:32.251 --> 11:33.591
What are you getting at?

11:33.671 --> 11:35.631
All that stuff about
the Cradle of Old Ragna,

11:35.721 --> 11:37.261
the Corridor of Memories?

11:37.341 --> 11:38.761
You sure are saying
an awful lot for someone

11:38.841 --> 11:40.721
who hasn't told me
anything yet!

11:40.801 --> 11:43.311
[splosh]

11:45.181 --> 11:46.521
Yeah, you're right.

11:46.601 --> 11:48.101
I'll keep it simple.

11:48.191 --> 11:51.061
This is a special place
where souls are filtered.

11:51.151 --> 11:54.151
What do you mean by filtered?

11:54.231 --> 11:56.441
You know how after you're
done using a washrag,

11:56.531 --> 11:58.821
you wash it, dry it,
and use it again?

11:58.901 --> 12:01.161
Well, the same kind of thing
happens for souls.

12:01.241 --> 12:03.741
All the dirt and filth
clinging to them is removed.

12:03.831 --> 12:04.991
That way they're
all squeaky clean

12:05.081 --> 12:06.661
so they can be used again.

12:06.751 --> 12:09.671
And this dirt and filth you're
talking about in this case

12:09.751 --> 12:12.211
is stuff like memories
and experiences?

12:12.291 --> 12:14.711
If that's the easiest way
for you to understand it,

12:14.801 --> 12:16.551
then sure, let's go with that.

12:16.631 --> 12:19.801
In fact, you can think
whatever you want, Mister.

12:19.881 --> 12:22.431
A filter for memories.

12:23.681 --> 12:24.601
I get it.

12:24.681 --> 12:29.271
♪ ♪

12:29.351 --> 12:35.401
So then the one who took all my
memories from before yesterday,

12:35.481 --> 12:37.111
it was you.

12:37.191 --> 12:38.191
You get it.

12:38.281 --> 12:39.651
[gasps]

12:39.741 --> 12:43.071
[clatter]

12:44.121 --> 12:45.491
Something's approaching
the tower.

12:45.581 --> 12:46.871
Gotta go!

12:46.951 --> 12:48.541
Take good care of Master
while I'm gone!

12:49.871 --> 12:52.791
[frenetic music]

12:53.711 --> 12:54.841
I'm going with her.

12:54.921 --> 12:57.011
Miss Emilia, Rem.

12:58.421 --> 13:00.421
Beatrice, stay with Subaru.

13:00.511 --> 13:01.881
Leave it to me, I suppose.

13:01.971 --> 13:09.061
♪ ♪

13:10.351 --> 13:13.271
[rumbling, growling]

13:13.351 --> 13:20.441
♪ ♪

13:25.201 --> 13:26.701
Infinite--

13:26.781 --> 13:30.201
[zapping, cranking]

13:30.291 --> 13:34.171
♪ ♪

13:34.251 --> 13:36.211
Hell's Snipe!

13:36.291 --> 13:42.631
♪ ♪

13:42.721 --> 13:46.011
[explosion]

13:46.101 --> 13:49.521
[rumbling, growling]

13:49.601 --> 13:53.481
♪ ♪

13:53.561 --> 13:56.611
[crashing]

13:57.521 --> 13:58.611
No.

14:06.161 --> 14:08.621
[sploshing]

14:12.581 --> 14:15.251
It's been a while,
Big Sister.

14:17.841 --> 14:22.421
In the end, this entire planet
runs on gluttony!

14:22.511 --> 14:25.431
[clatter]

14:25.511 --> 14:28.851
[dramatic music]

14:28.931 --> 14:31.021
♪ ♪

14:31.101 --> 14:32.681
[SUBARU] I get it.

14:32.771 --> 14:35.901
That means yesterday's me must
have stood in this same place.

14:35.981 --> 14:37.441
So clever.

14:37.521 --> 14:40.771
But it really is amazing,
truly wonderful for you

14:40.861 --> 14:42.611
to come back here again.

14:42.691 --> 14:44.071
So how many times
did it take for you

14:44.151 --> 14:45.571
to finally make it here?

14:46.951 --> 14:50.161
Come on, we really
want to know, Mister.

14:50.241 --> 14:53.251
We answered all of your
questions, so it's only fair.

14:53.331 --> 14:54.791
Hurry and tell us already.

14:54.871 --> 14:57.581
After we ate you up yesterday,
how many times

14:57.671 --> 14:59.501
did it take you to get here?

15:01.551 --> 15:04.421
[SUBARU] That's not something
I can talk about.

15:04.511 --> 15:05.841
Don't worry.

15:05.921 --> 15:07.721
What happens in here
doesn't make it outside,

15:07.801 --> 15:09.431
so you're totally safe.

15:09.511 --> 15:12.641
That means it can stay a secret
just between you and us.

15:12.721 --> 15:14.231
Well, Mister?

15:14.311 --> 15:15.891
How many times?

15:18.021 --> 15:19.861
This one is five.

15:21.231 --> 15:22.321
Amazing.

15:22.401 --> 15:24.071
That's amazing.
Just amazing.

15:24.151 --> 15:25.071
So amazing.

15:25.151 --> 15:26.071
Truly amazing.

15:26.151 --> 15:27.321
Absolutely amazing.

15:27.401 --> 15:29.451
Breathtakingly,
admirably amazing!

15:29.531 --> 15:32.161
That's exactly what
makes it gluttony.

15:34.911 --> 15:37.371
Until our stomach
is full to bursting,

15:37.461 --> 15:39.251
we want to take
our time savoring

15:39.331 --> 15:41.591
everything that makes you you.

15:41.671 --> 15:42.841
We've learned
something interesting

15:42.921 --> 15:44.511
from our experience.

15:44.591 --> 15:47.671
Hunger and lust are actually
very similar things, we think.

15:47.761 --> 15:50.721
And lust, well,
that's the same as love, right?

15:50.801 --> 15:52.851
In other words,
that means we must really,

15:52.931 --> 15:55.471
really love you, Mister.

15:55.561 --> 15:56.771
Stop it!

15:56.851 --> 15:58.981
[dramatic music]

16:00.441 --> 16:03.151
You better give me back
my memories or I'm going to--

16:03.231 --> 16:04.821
Strangle us?

16:04.901 --> 16:06.321
You'd do that to us?

16:06.401 --> 16:08.781
Wring the neck
of a sweet little girl?

16:08.861 --> 16:10.741
Do you think you can
actually go through

16:10.821 --> 16:12.531
with something like that?

16:12.621 --> 16:15.661
Besides, if doing that brings
Subaru Natsuki's memories back,

16:15.751 --> 16:17.701
then the person you are right
now will be lost forever,

16:17.791 --> 16:18.911
you know.

16:20.121 --> 16:21.581
It's obvious, isn't it?

16:21.671 --> 16:23.251
If those memories
return to you,

16:23.341 --> 16:24.631
then your current self
will simply

16:24.711 --> 16:26.801
be overwritten and disappear.

16:26.881 --> 16:29.881
And that's the same as dying,
don't you think?

16:29.971 --> 16:32.091
Is bringing that
Subaru Natsuki back

16:32.181 --> 16:35.351
really worth throwing
everything else away?

16:35.431 --> 16:37.311
What are you spewing now?

16:37.391 --> 16:40.941
Memories are what make
people who they are.

16:41.021 --> 16:43.401
See, Mister?

16:43.481 --> 16:44.981
The person you are
right now has

16:45.071 --> 16:47.361
created connections to others
that the old version

16:47.441 --> 16:48.401
doesn't have.

16:48.491 --> 16:51.321
♪ ♪

16:51.401 --> 16:54.281
And also, maybe it's
not right to say this,

16:54.371 --> 16:56.581
but let's be honest here.

16:56.661 --> 16:59.081
There's no need to pretend
that Subaru Natsuki is any kind

16:59.161 --> 17:01.121
of ideal man or anything.

17:02.461 --> 17:04.751
I feel so awful for Emilia.

17:04.841 --> 17:06.801
That poor silver-haired girl
had the misfortune

17:06.881 --> 17:08.761
of her birth being the same
as that which of old,

17:08.841 --> 17:11.091
and because of that,
everyone avoids her.

17:11.171 --> 17:15.221
I'm such a good, kind person
for wanting to be by your side.

17:15.301 --> 17:18.681
Then there's the weak, fragile
Beatrice, that sad, painfully

17:18.771 --> 17:20.431
lonely little girl
who spent all those years

17:20.521 --> 17:23.481
in solitude with
no one to rely on but herself.

17:23.561 --> 17:25.861
I know that this path
is dark and dangerous.

17:25.941 --> 17:27.901
I'll take her by the hand
and drag her with me.

17:27.981 --> 17:29.281
Oh, my god.

17:29.361 --> 17:31.401
And Rem, who devoted
all of her love to me

17:31.491 --> 17:34.741
and got nothing in return,
an incomplete being who

17:34.821 --> 17:37.371
tries her best to feel alive
by throwing herself away

17:37.451 --> 17:39.451
and desperately serving others.

17:39.541 --> 17:41.831
I have to be the one to play
the knight in shining armor

17:41.911 --> 17:43.161
and lead her.

17:43.251 --> 17:44.631
What are you getting at?

17:44.711 --> 17:46.711
All we're doing is
sharing the inner thoughts

17:46.791 --> 17:48.421
of Subaru Natsuki.

17:48.501 --> 17:51.301
What he wanted was
a sense of superiority.

17:51.381 --> 17:53.381
He had all those useful
people following him

17:53.471 --> 17:55.091
and got drunk
on feeling like he was

17:55.181 --> 17:57.511
the one doing them a service.

17:57.601 --> 17:59.261
You really want
to step aside

17:59.351 --> 18:01.471
and throw yourself away
for a Subaru Natsuki

18:01.561 --> 18:03.311
who had thoughts like those?

18:05.401 --> 18:07.271
Well?

18:07.361 --> 18:09.231
So what'll it be?

18:09.321 --> 18:11.481
How about it?
What will you do?

18:11.571 --> 18:14.701
You can do anything you want,
consequences be damned.

18:14.781 --> 18:16.781
Whatever you want to do,
it's fine.

18:16.871 --> 18:18.951
No matter what you do,
no matter how horrible,

18:19.041 --> 18:20.411
we'll forgive you.

18:20.491 --> 18:24.251
Now then, what are you
going to do, huh, Mister?

18:24.331 --> 18:26.251
Are you capable
of making a decision

18:26.331 --> 18:29.131
as important as this one?

18:29.211 --> 18:32.211
Try loving yourself more
seriously, more diligently,

18:32.301 --> 18:33.801
more genuinely.

18:33.881 --> 18:36.181
You should take up the most
space in your own heart.

18:36.261 --> 18:39.311
[pulsing heartbeat]

18:49.151 --> 18:50.271
I can't.

18:52.231 --> 18:55.651
I want to be

18:55.741 --> 18:59.201
with them more.

19:00.621 --> 19:02.541
We get it. Don't worry.

19:02.621 --> 19:04.201
We know exactly how you feel.

19:04.291 --> 19:05.501
We understand.

19:05.581 --> 19:06.711
[SUBARU] No.

19:08.581 --> 19:09.791
No.

19:11.251 --> 19:14.301
[zapping, cranking]

19:20.101 --> 19:20.971
No.

19:21.061 --> 19:24.471
[dramatic music]

19:24.561 --> 19:27.941
♪ ♪

19:28.021 --> 19:28.981
No.

19:29.061 --> 19:36.151
♪ ♪

19:38.781 --> 19:39.741
No.

19:39.821 --> 19:42.871
♪ ♪

19:42.951 --> 19:44.291
I don't want this.

19:44.371 --> 19:45.541
[RUI] No, you don't.

19:45.621 --> 19:47.041
That's right.

19:47.121 --> 19:49.461
So what do you think
you should do?

19:51.341 --> 19:54.301
I'll stay who I am.

19:54.381 --> 19:56.131
[RUI] Yes, you're going
to simply stay

19:56.211 --> 19:57.631
who you are, Mister.

19:57.721 --> 19:59.841
You took this life
for yourself once.

19:59.931 --> 20:01.601
It's a game of musical chairs.

20:01.681 --> 20:04.851
The one who takes
the final seat is the king.

20:04.931 --> 20:07.981
Now, acknowledge it--
out of everyone,

20:08.061 --> 20:11.561
Subaru Natsuki is the stranger
who's closest to you.

20:11.651 --> 20:13.021
So you should kill him.

20:13.111 --> 20:14.071
Just do it already.

20:14.151 --> 20:15.611
Kill him.
You have to.

20:15.691 --> 20:16.651
Do it.

20:16.741 --> 20:17.741
Let's kill him.

20:17.821 --> 20:18.821
Kill him off.

20:18.901 --> 20:20.201
It's your right to kill him.

20:20.281 --> 20:22.411
Kill him and end this.

20:22.491 --> 20:24.031
Subaru Natsuki.

20:24.121 --> 20:26.081
You are the one
and only Subaru Natsuki

20:26.161 --> 20:27.201
In this whole world.

20:27.291 --> 20:28.661
You are no one's replacement.

20:28.751 --> 20:30.211
You are Subaru Natsuki!

20:30.291 --> 20:31.211
You!

20:31.291 --> 20:34.421
[moaning]

20:37.511 --> 20:40.511
[footsteps]

20:45.431 --> 20:47.061
Did you get scared
and decide to come out

20:47.141 --> 20:50.061
and face me, Subaru Natsuki?

20:50.141 --> 20:52.401
I don't want to disappear.

20:52.481 --> 20:53.861
I don't want to die.

20:53.941 --> 20:57.071
I want to stay and keep
being with everyone.

20:57.151 --> 20:58.821
I love them all so much.

20:58.901 --> 21:00.531
That's why.

21:00.611 --> 21:03.951
That's why I know that I
couldn't possibly be you.

21:04.031 --> 21:05.121
We're so different.

21:05.201 --> 21:06.621
Are you talking to someone?

21:06.701 --> 21:08.161
Who, Mister?

21:08.241 --> 21:09.701
There's no one else here.

21:09.791 --> 21:10.751
So who?

21:10.831 --> 21:13.331
[footsteps]

21:14.211 --> 21:17.341
[footsteps stop]

21:23.721 --> 21:28.011
Tell me, why have you decided
it must be one or the other?

21:31.771 --> 21:33.651
[sniffs]

21:33.731 --> 21:36.571
[bright music]

21:36.651 --> 21:41.441
♪ ♪

21:41.531 --> 21:42.611
Stand up!

21:45.951 --> 21:47.331
Huh?

21:47.411 --> 21:49.331
Stand up now!

21:49.411 --> 21:52.661
Why do you have to talk
to me like that

21:52.751 --> 21:54.831
on top of everything else
happening?

21:54.921 --> 21:56.251
Stand up!

21:56.341 --> 21:57.881
Who are you?

21:57.961 --> 22:00.001
Isn't this enough already?

22:00.091 --> 22:02.381
It took a lot
for me to decide this.

22:02.471 --> 22:04.181
Stand up!

22:04.261 --> 22:08.221
Even though it's been
so hard, so painful.

22:08.311 --> 22:09.431
Why?

22:10.641 --> 22:12.021
Stand up!
Stand up!

22:12.101 --> 22:13.021
Stand up!

22:13.101 --> 22:14.191
On your feet!

22:15.521 --> 22:22.361
Why-- why does my chest
feel like it's on fire?

22:23.281 --> 22:25.031
Stand up, Subaru Natsuki!

22:25.111 --> 22:27.411
[inspirational music]

22:27.491 --> 22:28.781
Get on your feet!

22:29.991 --> 22:31.201
You're my hero!

22:31.291 --> 22:38.341
♪ ♪

22:40.591 --> 22:43.551
If you can stand, then go.

22:43.631 --> 22:44.551
Go.

22:44.631 --> 22:45.721
Save them.

22:45.801 --> 22:47.891
Save them all.

22:47.971 --> 22:50.181
How am I supposed to do that?

22:50.261 --> 22:52.101
What does that even mean?

22:52.181 --> 22:53.731
All means all.

22:53.811 --> 22:58.401
Everything-- all people,
yourself, and the stranger

22:58.481 --> 22:59.981
who's closest to you.

23:02.321 --> 23:04.781
But am I even capable?

23:04.861 --> 23:07.201
Do you seriously think
that I can do that?

23:09.531 --> 23:11.331
Yes, I do.

23:11.411 --> 23:12.621
Because--

23:12.701 --> 23:15.001
Because-- because why?

23:15.081 --> 23:16.001
Help me.

23:16.081 --> 23:17.371
Just give me an answer!

23:17.461 --> 23:19.291
In your own words, tell me.

23:19.381 --> 23:22.501
[soft music]

23:22.591 --> 23:28.841
Because you're--
you're still the same.

23:28.931 --> 23:30.351
You're my hero, Subaru.

23:30.431 --> 23:34.771
♪ ♪

23:34.851 --> 23:38.351
[sparkling tinkle]

23:39.231 --> 23:42.151
[dramatic music]

23:42.231 --> 23:49.321
♪ ♪

23:53.041 --> 23:55.291
Hey, Mister?

23:55.371 --> 23:57.541
What's the matter, huh?

23:57.621 --> 23:59.881
If you're done,
let's continue with what we--

23:59.961 --> 24:01.841
[SUBARU] You don't need
to say another word to me.

24:01.921 --> 24:03.051
Huh?

24:04.551 --> 24:05.881
I've had just
about enough of you

24:05.961 --> 24:08.341
and your disgusting,
sick manipulation.

24:11.141 --> 24:13.181
Just one step away.

24:13.261 --> 24:14.811
We were so close.

24:14.891 --> 24:17.681
Just one little step away from
tearing Subaru Natsuki

24:17.771 --> 24:19.401
and Subaru Natsuki apart.

24:19.481 --> 24:20.521
What the hell?

24:20.601 --> 24:22.061
Why would you be trying to--

24:22.151 --> 24:23.691
Because it's impossible
for us

24:23.771 --> 24:24.981
to eat the same person twice.

24:25.071 --> 24:26.691
Come on, isn't that obvious?

24:26.781 --> 24:29.151
That's why we needed you
to stay separated so badly.

24:29.241 --> 24:31.821
The Subaru Natsuki we ate
and the Subaru Natsuki

24:31.911 --> 24:33.951
we didn't had to stay
separate for it to work out.

24:34.031 --> 24:35.451
You're ruining everything!

24:35.541 --> 24:38.161
Then what exactly is it
that you guys want?

24:38.251 --> 24:40.831
To be happy,
plain and simple.

24:40.921 --> 24:43.711
Tell me, Mister, have you
ever thought to yourself

24:43.791 --> 24:45.211
that life was unfair?

24:45.301 --> 24:46.961
You can't choose
where you're born.

24:47.051 --> 24:48.761
You can't choose who
your parents are.

24:48.841 --> 24:50.511
You can't choose
your environment.

24:50.591 --> 24:51.971
You can't choose your future.

24:52.051 --> 24:53.801
You can't choose
anything at all.

24:53.891 --> 24:57.061
So then what if you
could choose?

24:57.141 --> 25:00.141
That's why we search
for that possibility--

25:00.231 --> 25:02.651
a rose-colored future
that makes us glad

25:02.731 --> 25:04.271
we lived this life.

25:04.361 --> 25:07.321
And until we find that perfect
life that's fated for us,

25:07.401 --> 25:09.861
we keep eating, keep biting,
keep licking,

25:09.941 --> 25:14.241
keep sucking, keep devouring,
keep gluttonizing.

25:14.321 --> 25:17.041
But no matter how many other
people's memories we eat,

25:17.121 --> 25:19.371
we can't get that
sweet memory of death.

25:19.451 --> 25:21.081
And that leaves us hungry.

25:21.161 --> 25:23.961
Come on, tell us
what it feels like to die?

25:24.041 --> 25:25.631
It must be painful, right?

25:25.711 --> 25:27.091
Agonizing, right?

25:27.171 --> 25:28.211
Terrible, right?

25:28.301 --> 25:29.381
It hurts, right?

25:29.461 --> 25:30.801
Some people even say
it feels good.

25:30.881 --> 25:31.801
Is that true?

25:31.881 --> 25:33.511
Hey, what's it like?

25:33.591 --> 25:35.011
Tell us right now.

25:35.101 --> 25:36.971
Thought you took all
my memories from yesterday.

25:37.061 --> 25:38.641
Shouldn't you know
that already?

25:38.721 --> 25:41.351
But those are old and dusty,
and they're not real.

25:41.431 --> 25:44.351
We want to experience
a newer, fresher sensation.

25:44.441 --> 25:46.691
A juicy, succulent new frontier
that no one else

25:46.771 --> 25:47.941
has ever known.

25:48.031 --> 25:49.731
A super special,
one-of-a-kind memory

25:49.821 --> 25:52.821
that no other person in this
world could experience.

25:52.901 --> 25:55.451
If you make a mistake and want
to redo, all you have to do

25:55.531 --> 25:57.621
is die and you can start over.

25:57.701 --> 25:59.791
Even if someone finds
their perfect life,

25:59.871 --> 26:01.501
there's a chance they could
make one little mistake

26:01.581 --> 26:04.211
and boom, all gone, you know?

26:04.291 --> 26:05.751
But you're different, Mister.

26:05.831 --> 26:08.381
That's not how
your life works at all.

26:08.461 --> 26:10.591
So please, we're begging.

26:10.671 --> 26:12.761
Your life seems so tasty.

26:15.221 --> 26:17.011
Won't you let us have our fill?

26:17.101 --> 26:18.511
We're so hungry.

26:21.101 --> 26:22.641
The suffering
I've been through,

26:22.731 --> 26:25.811
the death and everything
in my life, they belong to me

26:25.901 --> 26:27.061
and me alone.

26:27.151 --> 26:28.651
Oh!

26:28.731 --> 26:30.691
I'm keeping them, so I don't
have a single damn thing

26:30.781 --> 26:32.111
to give to you.

26:32.191 --> 26:33.491
Want to dine and dash?

26:33.571 --> 26:34.701
Just starve.

26:35.951 --> 26:37.161
Hate to break it to you,

26:37.241 --> 26:39.121
but this isn't going to
go your way.

26:39.201 --> 26:41.581
My name is Subaru Natsuki.

26:41.661 --> 26:44.001
Kenichi Natsuki and
Naoko Natsuki are the ones

26:44.081 --> 26:45.791
who gave it to me
when I was born.

26:45.871 --> 26:47.381
I'm not anything else.

26:47.461 --> 26:48.711
I'm me.

26:48.791 --> 26:50.501
Even though you might
be overwritten

26:50.591 --> 26:52.051
and disappear for good?

26:52.131 --> 26:54.471
I'm going to teach you some
magic words that'll help.

26:54.551 --> 26:57.261
"That's one thing,
and this is another."

26:59.851 --> 27:02.101
[RUI] Ah, we failed!

27:02.181 --> 27:03.431
We messed it all up!

27:03.521 --> 27:04.561
We ruined everything!

27:04.641 --> 27:05.601
We screwed up!

27:05.691 --> 27:07.651
We botched it! We blew it!

27:07.731 --> 27:10.191
We're just going to have to
leave the rest to Big Brother

27:10.271 --> 27:12.281
and Brother Dearest then.

27:12.361 --> 27:14.651
Does that mean your brothers
are in this tower, too?

27:14.741 --> 27:15.701
I've gotta move.

27:15.781 --> 27:16.901
Eh?

27:17.781 --> 27:20.161
[clatter, crash]

27:21.331 --> 27:22.491
What's this?

27:22.581 --> 27:24.251
It's the exit
that leads out of here.

27:24.331 --> 27:25.961
What are you going
to do, Mister?

27:28.291 --> 27:29.961
You're going to regret this.

27:30.041 --> 27:31.881
See, Big Brother
and Brother Dearest

27:31.961 --> 27:35.171
have big appetites that
they'll do anything to satisfy.

27:38.641 --> 27:40.141
It'll be fine.

27:40.221 --> 27:41.811
I remember our promise.

27:43.561 --> 27:46.771
There's nothing that could
make Subaru Natsuki forget it.

27:46.851 --> 27:49.561
So I know we will meet again.

27:49.651 --> 27:53.321
And next time, don't use the
harsh tone of voice with me.

27:53.401 --> 27:55.741
Let me hear your gentle
voice instead.

27:59.621 --> 28:03.541
Argh, damn it all!

28:03.621 --> 28:05.411
I can't believe this guy.

28:05.491 --> 28:08.831
[SUBARU] Stop it, Rui!

28:08.921 --> 28:11.001
What the hell are you doing?

28:12.501 --> 28:14.591
What is this
strange sensation?

28:14.671 --> 28:16.051
This love?

28:16.131 --> 28:19.551
Something so dear
anchored deep in your heart.

28:19.641 --> 28:21.011
I won't let this go.

28:21.091 --> 28:22.601
You will not escape.

28:22.681 --> 28:23.601
Never!

28:23.681 --> 28:24.851
Never, ever!

28:24.931 --> 28:28.311
[dramatic music]

28:28.391 --> 28:32.271
♪ ♪

28:32.361 --> 28:34.651
We threw away
those pesky memories.

28:34.731 --> 28:38.151
All that's left now
is to eat you all up.

28:38.241 --> 28:39.571
Don't think
we're going to just let this

28:39.651 --> 28:42.661
be over, Subaru Natsuki.

28:42.741 --> 28:46.991
♪ ♪

28:47.081 --> 28:50.501
[gasping]

28:52.791 --> 28:54.881
Everything that makes up
your life,

28:54.961 --> 28:58.551
all of it belongs to us!
